  "body": {
    "id": "thm:universal-lower",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every fixed \\(K\\), every \\(c\\in\\mathcal C_K\\), every outcome-independent possibly dependent design \\(\\mathcal D_n\\), and every possibly randomized full-label estimator \\(\\widehat\\tau\\), \\[\\max_{y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times K}}\\mathcal R_n(\\mathcal D_n,\\widehat\\tau;y)\\ge n^{-1}-\\Lambda_cn^{-4/3}+o(n^{-4/3}),\\] where the remainder is uniform over the design-estimator pair. Equivalently, \\(\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}n^{4/3}d_n(c)\\le\\Lambda_c\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "K",
      "c",
      "\\mathcal C_K",
      "\\mathcal D_n",
      "\\widehat\\tau",
      "y",
      "n",
      "\\mathcal R_n(\\mathcal D_n,\\widehat\\tau;y)",
      "\\Lambda_c",
      "d_n(c)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:fixed-arm-count",
      "ass:zero-sum-contrast",
      "ass:l1-normalization",
      "ass:three-active-arms",
      "ass:outcome-independent-design",
      "def:risk-game",
      "thm:all-count-bayes-inequality"
    ],
    "justification": "The lower theorem turns a fixed-design Airy expansion into an unrestricted joint design-estimation result.",
    "gap": "Bai (2023), Theorem 2.1, supplies permutation symmetrization; Theorem 3.1 reduces the minimax problem over linear-estimator schemes to minimization of a quadratic expected form subject to linear constraints over assignment and estimator schemes; and Theorem 3.2 gives the two-arm complete-randomization, Neyman-allocation, and difference-in-means specialization. These results do not control arbitrary binary schedules, nonlinear estimators, or the second-order coefficient.",
    "consumer": "The theorem rules out correlated or face-supported randomizations that could otherwise invalidate the proposed clinical allocation.",
    "proof_tex": "Use the priors supplied by the resolved all-count Bayes theorem.  For any outcome-independent design \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) and any randomized estimator \\(\\widehat\\tau\\), averaging its risk under \\(\\Pi_n\\) gives\n\\[\n\\max_y\\mathcal R_n(\\mathcal D_n,\\widehat\\tau;y)\n\\ge\\sum_y\\Pi_n(y)\\mathcal R_n(\\mathcal D_n,\\widehat\\tau;y).\n\\]\nCondition on the full assignment vector.  Outcome independence makes its law independent of \\(y\\), and conditional Jensen inequality shows that auxiliary estimator randomization cannot lower posterior squared error below the posterior mean.  Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_y\\Pi_n(y)\\mathcal R_n(\\mathcal D_n,\\widehat\\tau;y)\n\\ge\\sum_z\\mathcal D_n(z)\\operatorname{BR}_{\\Pi_n}(r(z))\n\\ge\\inf_r\\operatorname{BR}_{\\Pi_n}(r).\n\\]\nThe all-count theorem therefore yields\n\\[\n\\max_y\\mathcal R_n(\\mathcal D_n,\\widehat\\tau;y)\n\\ge n^{-1}-\\Lambda_cn^{-4/3}-o(n^{-4/3}),\n\\]\nwhere the remainder is independent of both the design and the estimator.  Taking the infimum over the pair proves the corresponding lower bound for \\(R_n(c)\\).  Since \\(d_n(c)=n^{-1}-R_n(c)\\), rearrangement gives\n\\(\\limsup_n n^{4/3}d_n(c)\\le\\Lambda_c\\).",
